SINA_SMART["anat3-eye-innervation"] = {"lecture":"anat3-eye-innervation","title":"Motor and sensory innervation of the eye and its appendages","mapStatus":"auto: generated by universal.py from the lecture summary and key points; not human-reviewed","los":[{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:s0","text":"Describe and explain: The big picture","topic":"The big picture","sec":0,"importance":"standard","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:s1","text":"Describe and explain: What the lecture asks you to do","topic":"What the lecture asks you to do","sec":1,"importance":"standard","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e1","text":"The optic nerve is wrapped in meninges with CSF around it.","topic":"Cranial nerves at a glance","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e2","text":"Visual pathway orders: photoreceptors, bipolar cells, then the geniculate to cortex neuron.","topic":"Cranial nerves at a glance","sec":2,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e3","text":"Abducens nucleus: motor neurons for the ipsilateral lateral rectus plus interneurons crossing to the contralateral oculomotor nucleus.","topic":"Motor supply and eye movement","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e4","text":"MLF produces conjugate eye movement and coordinates eyes with head rotation.","topic":"Motor supply and eye movement","sec":3,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e15","text":"Photopic (cones): bright light, colour, detail, low light sensitivity. Scotopic (rods): dim light, no colour, high light sensitivity.","topic":"Accommodation","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e5","text":"Accommodation: ciliary muscle contracts, suspensory ligament relaxes, lens more convex, dioptric power increases.","topic":"Accommodation","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e6","text":"Far vision at rest: ciliary muscle relaxed, ligaments taut, lens flat.","topic":"Accommodation","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e7","text":"Near response: accommodation, pupil change, convergence.","topic":"Accommodation","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e8","text":"Accommodation reflex afferent: retina to visual cortex. Efferent: oculomotor nucleus, ciliary ganglion, ciliary muscle.","topic":"Accommodation","sec":4,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e10","text":"Light reflex efferent: CN III, ciliary ganglion, short ciliary nerves, sphincter pupillae.","topic":"The pupillary light reflex","sec":5,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e11","text":"Bilateral projections give the direct and consensual responses; darkness inhibits the reflex, giving dilation.","topic":"The pupillary light reflex","sec":5,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e9","text":"Light reflex afferent: retina, optic nerve, chiasm, tract, pretectal nuclei, both Edinger-Westphal nuclei.","topic":"The pupillary light reflex","sec":5,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e12","text":"Corneal reflex: afferent V1 to the spinal nucleus of V; efferent facial motor nucleus both sides, CN VII, orbicularis oculi.","topic":"The corneal reflex","sec":6,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e0","text":"128 sensory, 346/11/12 motor, 5-7-9-10 mixed; parasympathetic in 3, 7, 9, 10.","topic":"Autonomic control of the lacrimal gland","sec":7,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e13","text":"Lacrimal gland: parasympathetic through the greater superficial petrosal nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion stimulates; sympathetic through the deep petrosal nerve inhibits.","topic":"Autonomic control of the lacrimal gland","sec":7,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"},{"id":"anat3-eye-innervation:e14","text":"Ciliary ganglion: parasympathetic synapse, sympathetic and sensory pass through; branches are the short ciliary nerves.","topic":"Autonomic control of the lacrimal gland","sec":7,"importance":"core","highYield":"untested"}],"vmap":{},"mlevel":{"1i6fns5":"a","1s6ziur":"a","n5ty4":"a","12dg8qj":"a","ph9rum":"a","1t6irmx":"a","ige592":"a","w4lfuh":"a","ogninj":"a","1mctj2k":"a","1oqio2s":"a","1gzfhi":"a","pa6165":"a","19zehjn":"a","11n7klh":"a","1dy0obm":"a","tuiuaf":"a","1efh14l":"a","1dkkfqi":"a","1x4vt1h":"a","1ltbouc":"a","1nvdoy4":"a","g3v2y5":"a","em338y":"a"},"mapCounts":{"verified":0,"auto":24,"ambiguous":0,"missing":0},"mrules":[],"items":[],"experimental":[],"status":{},"difficulty":{},"expl":{"anat3-eye-innervation:s0":{"s":0,"u":["This lecture is about wiring.","Which nerves reach the eye, and what circuits they form."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:s1":{"s":1,"u":["Discuss the cranial nerves associated with the eye.","Relate the visual pathway to image formation."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e1":{"s":2,"u":["The optic nerve is wrapped in meninges with CSF around it.","Unlike other cranial nerves it is wrapped in the meninges: dura, subdural space, arachnoid, subarachnoid space containing CSF, and pia.","The chain: cones and rods, bipolar cells, ganglion cells, optic nerve, optic chiasma, optic tract, lateral geniculate body in the thalamus, geniculocalcarine tract, optic radiation, visual cortex (Brodmann area 17) in the occipital cortex."],"dp":[{"h":"Why papilloedema is visible in the eye","t":"The lecture's slide showing CSF around the optic nerve explains a classic sign. Because the subarachnoid space continues along the nerve right up to the eyeball, a rise in intracranial pressure is transmitted down that sleeve and pushes the optic disc forward. That swelling, papilloedema, is visible with an ophthalmoscope, which is why doctors look in the eye when they suspect raised pressure in the skull. Source: the lecture's own diagram of the meningeal sheaths and CSF, read with the visual pathway slides."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e2":{"s":2,"u":["Visual pathway orders: photoreceptors, bipolar cells, then the geniculate to cortex neuron.","Orders of neuron in the visual pathway: first order, the cone and rod photoreceptors; second order, the bipolar cells leading to the ganglion cells of the optic nerve; third order, the neuron from the lateral geniculate body to the cerebral cortex.","The chain: cones and rods, bipolar cells, ganglion cells, optic nerve, optic chiasma, optic tract, lateral geniculate body in the thalamus, geniculocalcarine tract, optic radiation, visual cortex (Brodmann area 17) in the occipital cortex."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e3":{"s":3,"u":["Abducens nucleus: motor neurons for the ipsilateral lateral rectus plus interneurons crossing to the contralateral oculomotor nucleus.","The abducens nucleus contains two types of cell: motor neurons that control the lateral rectus muscle on the same side, and interneurons that cross the midline and connect to the contralateral oculomotor nucleus , which controls the medial rectus of the opposite eye."],"dp":[{"h":"Internuclear ophthalmoplegia: the MLF in practice","t":"The crossing interneurons of the abducens nucleus and the MLF are what let both eyes look sideways together. Cut the MLF between the two, and on looking to one side the abducting eye still moves while the adducting eye lags behind, even though the medial rectus works normally for convergence. This dissociation is a well-known sign of a brainstem lesion, and the lecture's abducens slide is exactly the anatomy behind it. Source: the lecture's abducens nucleus and MLF slides."},{"h":"Note","t":"Abducens nucleus has motor neurons (ipsilateral LR) and interneurons crossing in the MLF to the opposite CN III nucleus (contralateral MR): this produces conjugate horizontal gaze ."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e4":{"s":3,"u":["MLF produces conjugate eye movement and coordinates eyes with head rotation.","It links CN III, CN IV, CN VI and CN VIII with the cerebellum, producing conjugate movement of the eyes and coordinating them with head movement.","Medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e15":{"s":4,"u":["Photopic (cones): bright light, colour, detail, low light sensitivity. Scotopic (rods): dim light, no colour, high light sensitivity.","Sensitivity: Cones (photopic) = Low sensitivity to light: needs a high visual threshold to be stimulated; Rods (scotopic) = Great sensitivity to light: low visual threshold.","Light: Cones (photopic) = Bright light vision; Rods (scotopic) = Night and dim light vision."],"dp":[{"h":"Clinical","t":"Relative afferent pupillary defect (swinging-light test): swinging the light to the eye with a damaged optic nerve makes both pupils dilate , because less light signal gets in."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e5":{"s":4,"u":["Accommodation: ciliary muscle contracts, suspensory ligament relaxes, lens more convex, dioptric power increases.","The suspensory ligament relaxes: 2.","The dioptric power of the eye increases: 4."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e6":{"s":4,"u":["Far vision at rest: ciliary muscle relaxed, ligaments taut, lens flat.","The suspensory ligament contracts (taut).","The lens becomes less convex (flat)."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e7":{"s":4,"u":["Near response: accommodation, pupil change, convergence.","The near response has three linked parts: lens changes (accommodation), changes in the pupil, and convergence of the eyes.","The accommodation reflex."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e8":{"s":4,"u":["Accommodation reflex afferent: retina to visual cortex. Efferent: oculomotor nucleus, ciliary ganglion, ciliary muscle.","Efferent: oculomotor nucleus, parasympathetic fibres, ciliary ganglion, ciliary muscle, circular pupillary muscle.","Ciliary muscles contract: 1."],"dp":[{"h":"Note","t":"Accommodation reflex (near triad) : lens thickens + pupil constricts + convergence . Afferent goes all the way to the visual cortex ; efferent = CN III (parasympathetic to ciliary and sphincter muscles, somatic to medial recti)."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e10":{"s":5,"u":["Light reflex efferent: CN III, ciliary ganglion, short ciliary nerves, sphincter pupillae.","VII. short ciliary nerve.","Those postganglionic fibres, the short ciliary nerves , innervate the sphincter muscle of the pupil, producing pupillary constriction."],"dp":[{"h":"Note","t":"Light reflex : afferent = CN II → pretectal nuclei → both Edinger-Westphal nuclei; efferent = CN III → ciliary ganglion → short ciliary nerves → sphincter pupillae. Direct + consensual."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e11":{"s":5,"u":["Bilateral projections give the direct and consensual responses; darkness inhibits the reflex, giving dilation.","In darkness the reflex becomes inhibited, giving dilation of the pupil in both eyes.","Light shone in one eye constricts the pupil of that eye ( direct reflex) and of the other eye ( consensual reflex)."],"dp":[{"h":"Why the light reflex tests two different nerves","t":"The afferent limb is CN II and the efferent limb is CN III, so comparing the direct and consensual responses localises the fault. If the optic nerve of one eye is damaged, light in that eye constricts neither pupil, but light in the good eye still constricts both. If CN III is damaged, the affected pupil will not constrict to light in either eye, while the other pupil still does. Source: the lecture's afferent and efferent pathway key, applied to its direct and consensual definitions."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e9":{"s":5,"u":["Light reflex afferent: retina, optic nerve, chiasm, tract, pretectal nuclei, both Edinger-Westphal nuclei.","The pretectal nuclei project to the ipsilateral Edinger-Westphal nucleus and also to the contralateral one via the posterior commissure.","Fibres from the optic tracts project to and synapse in the pretectal nuclei in the dorsal midbrain, in the collicular region."],"dp":[]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e12":{"s":6,"u":["Corneal reflex: afferent V1 to the spinal nucleus of V; efferent facial motor nucleus both sides, CN VII, orbicularis oculi.","Touch on the cornea travels in the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve (V) to the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve , then to the facial motor nucleus on both sides .","The temporal branch of the facial nerve (VII) makes the orbicularis oculi contract, so both eyes blink."],"dp":[{"h":"Note","t":"Corneal reflex : afferent V1 (nasociliary) → spinal nucleus of V → both facial nuclei → efferent CN VII → orbicularis oculi. Touch one cornea → both eyes blink."},{"h":"Note","t":"In the corneal reflex, the side that fails to blink tells you which nerve is hit: no blink on either side when one cornea is touched = that side's V1 ; only one eye fails to blink whichever side is touched = that side's VII ."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e0":{"s":7,"u":["128 sensory, 346/11/12 motor, 5-7-9-10 mixed; parasympathetic in 3, 7, 9, 10.","The ciliary ganglion carries three kinds of fibre: parasympathetic (which synapse there, arriving by the motor root from the inferior division of CN III), sympathetic (from the carotid plexus) and sensory (from the nasociliary nerve)."],"dp":[{"h":"Memory aid","t":"Cranial nerve types: sensory 1, 2, 8 ; motor 3, 4, 6, 11, 12 ; mixed 5, 7, 9, 10 ; parasympathetic 3, 7, 9, 10 ('1973')."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e13":{"s":7,"u":["Lacrimal gland: parasympathetic through the greater superficial petrosal nerve and pterygopalatine ganglion stimulates; sympathetic through the deep petrosal nerve inhibits.","Parasympathetic: Pathway = Facial nerve, greater superficial petrosal nerve, pterygoid canal, pterygopalatine ganglion, then the zygomatic and lacrimal nerves to the gland; Effect = Stimulates secretion.","Sympathetic: Pathway = Internal carotid plexus, deep petrosal nerve, joining the same route through the pterygoid canal; Effect = Inhibits secretion."],"dp":[{"h":"Crocodile tears","t":"The parasympathetic fibres for tears and those for saliva both leave the facial nerve near the geniculate ganglion. If the nerve is injured and the fibres regrow along the wrong branch, salivary fibres can end up in the lacrimal gland. The person then produces tears when they eat rather than saliva. It is a direct consequence of the shared route shown on the lecture's lacrimal gland slide. Source: the lecture's autonomic control of the lacrimal gland diagram."}]},"anat3-eye-innervation:e14":{"s":7,"u":["Ciliary ganglion: parasympathetic synapse, sympathetic and sensory pass through; branches are the short ciliary nerves.","Its branches are the short ciliary nerves.","The sympathetic and sensory fibres pass straight through."],"dp":[{"h":"Note","t":"Ciliary ganglion : only the parasympathetic fibres (from CN III) synapse there; sympathetic and sensory fibres just pass through . Its output = short ciliary nerves."}]}},"meta":{"1i6fns5":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e14/recall/1"},"1s6ziur":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e0/recall/1"},"n5ty4":{"a":"relationship","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e1/relationship/1"},"12dg8qj":{"a":"relationship","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e2/relationship/1"},"ph9rum":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e8/recall/1"},"1t6irmx":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e3/recall/1"},"ige592":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e4/recall/1"},"w4lfuh":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e8/recall/2"},"ogninj":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e7/recall/1"},"1mctj2k":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e6/recall/1"},"1oqio2s":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e1/recall/1"},"1gzfhi":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e9/recall/1"},"pa6165":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e9/recall/2"},"19zehjn":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e10/recall/1"},"11n7klh":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e11/recall/1"},"1dy0obm":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e12/recall/1"},"tuiuaf":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e12/recall/2"},"1efh14l":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e15/recall/1"},"1dkkfqi":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e13/recall/1"},"1x4vt1h":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e14/recall/2"},"1ltbouc":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e8/recall/1"},"1nvdoy4":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e7/recall/2"},"g3v2y5":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e8/recall/3"},"em338y":{"a":"recall","vg":"anat3-eye-innervation:e15/recall/1"}},"explRev":"fd0a307fa71a"};
